      "abstract": "a similar relationship to the past as Sebald\u2019s narrators, for example, Gestermann makes the claim that the historical materialist\u2019s view of the past is primarily an individual and subjective one: \u201cDer unbestreitbare Fokus des historischen Materialismus auf eine Betrachtung der Vergangenheit, die sich am subjektiven Blick der Einzelperson ausrichtet, entspricht ganz der erz\u00e4hlerischen Haltung Sebalds und der Perspektive seiner personalen Erz\u00e4hlerinstanzen\u201d (71; similar claims appear throughout the book). The engagement with the past that Benjamin promotes, however, is clearly one meant to operate on a collective level. As Michael L\u00f6wy writes in Fire Alarm: Reading Walter Benjamin\u2019s \u201cOn the Concept of History,\u201d \u201cThe only possible Messiah is a collective one [ . . . ]. It is not a question of waiting for the Messiah [ . . . ] but of acting collectively\u201d (trans. Chris Turner, Verso 2005: 33). Moreover, in applying Benjamin\u2019s ideas to Sebald\u2019s works, Gestermann often elides a key distinction: that between the individual\u2019s memory of personal experiences and the collective memory of historical events in which individuals may participate. While it may be fruitful to move Benjamin\u2019s ideas about the commemoration of the historical past onto new ground by applying them to personal memory, not to acknowledge the act of translation this involves is to exaggerate the parallel between the two writers\u2019 subjects. Limited by the narrowness of its focus and by these faulty claims, Vergegenw\u00e4rtigung der Vergangenheit constitutes a minor contribution to scholarship on W.G. Sebald.",
